The attached image (Fig. 3) illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response and is taken up by recruited immune cells at the injection site. Panel A shows the temporal dynamics of various inflammatory cells, including macrophages and monocytes, peaking around day 2 post-injection, with neutrophils rapidly decreasing after day 1. Panel B presents confocal microscopy images, demonstrating the intracellular presence of Nano-11 (red) within MHCII+, Ly6C+, Mac-2+, and 1A8+ cells (green) from 6 hours to 7 days, indicating efficient uptake by these immune populations. This supports Nano-11's role in local immune activation and antigen delivery.